   "text": "This is a PROTAC-type molecule: a VHL ligand joined through a PEG linker to a simvastatin-derived hydroxy-lactone. I read it in three parts.\n\n- **VHL ligand:** a 4-methylthiazol-5-yl phenyl group on a benzylamide, attached to a 4-hydroxyproline. The proline nitrogen is acylated by a tert-leucine residue. I took the stereochemistry as the standard (2S,4R)-hydroxyproline and (S)-tert-leucine.\n- **Linker:** the tert-leucine NH carries a C(=O)CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013NH\u2013C(=O)CH2CH2C(CH3)2\u2013C(=O) chain, which ends in an ester.\n- **Statin end:** the ester oxygen sits on the hexahydronaphthalene ring of simvastatin. The ring has two methyl groups and a two-carbon chain to the 4-hydroxy-6-oxo-tetrahydropyran-2-yl lactone. I assigned the natural simvastatin stereochemistry, which is consistent with the wedges and hashes I could see.\n\nI did not independently re-derive each stereocentre from the wedges. The statin stereo tags come from the known simvastatin SMILES, and the proline and tert-leucine tags from the standard VHL ligand SMILES.\n\nSMILES: Cc1ncsc1-c1ccc(CNC(=O)[C@@H]2C[C@@H](O)CN2C(=O)[C@@H](NC(=O)COCCOCCNC(=O)CCC(C)(C)C(=O)O[C@H]3C[C@H](C=C4[C@H]3[C@H]([C@H](C=C4)C)CC[C@@H]5C[C@H](CC(=O)O5)O)C)C(C)(C)C)cc1"
